<UserControl
    x:Class="FaunaApp.Controls.ThreadHeader"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:loc="using:FaunaApp.Markup"
    xmlns:ids="using:FaunaApp.UiIds">
    <!--
        TWO ROWS, EACH WITH ITS OWN COLUMNS — never one shared column set.
        A Grid's columns are shared by every row, and the chips row spans all
        of them, so a chip wider than the pane fed its excess desired width
        into the shared `Auto` columns and starved the `*` title column: with a
        member whose home nest has not announced yet, the chip renders that
        member's FULL 64-hex actor id (`value-formatting.md` § Account display
        label — the elided form is the *resolved* fallback, not this one), the
        title column collapsed to **11 px**, the thread title wrapped into a
        548 px-tall tower of single characters, and `thread-room-class` /
        `thread-room-settings-button` were pushed clean out of the window —
        UIA-present (`count=1`) and `IsOffscreen`, which is what a driver reads
        as NOT VISIBLE. Measured on `test_conversation_room_ownership.py`'s
        outgoing-owner seat, 2026-09-11; it is a
        real user-facing bug, not a test artifact — a human on that seat sees a
        vertical title and no header buttons.

        Splitting the two rows into independent Grids removes the coupling at
        the root: neither row has a column-spanning child any more, so each
        sizes its own Autos and gives the rest to its own star column.

        The ROOM CLASS STATEMENT IS ITS OWN CAPTION LINE, under the title row —
        never an `Auto` column beside the title. A star column gets only what
        its row's Autos leave, and the detail pane at the app's own 600-DIP
        default window is ~266 px: with "End-to-end encrypted" (120 px) beside
        the icon and three 38 px buttons, row 0's Autos took all of it, the
        title column went to 0 px, and the title vanished — an empty rect,
        which UI Automation reports as IsOffscreen, so `thread-header` read NOT
        VISIBLE while every control beside it was on screen. Measured on
        `test_conversation_room_roles.py`'s owner seat, 2026-09-14. Android paints the class on its own line
        under the header row for the same narrow-width reason
        (`ConversationDetailScreen.kt`), and this is that shape.
    -->
    <Grid Padding="16">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- Row 0 — the title and the header controls. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <!-- thread-header id lives on the always-present label TextBlock (a realized
             UIA peer), NOT the UserControl root / a container — a WinUI UserControl
             root surfaces no queryable peer, so the e2e's is_visible("thread-header")
             counted 0 (reference_winui_paneheader_not_uia_realized; same lesson as the
             thread-member-chip-on-TextBlock note below). -->
        <!-- NoWrap + ellipsis is the belt behind the two-Grid fix above: a
             title too long for its column must lose its tail, never grow the
             header vertically (the 548 px tower). The full text stays readable
             to a driver — `get_text` reads the TextBlock's Text, not its
             rendered glyphs. -->
        <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" x:Name="ThreadLabel"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="{x:Bind ids:Ids.ThreadHeader}"
                   TextWrapping="NoWrap" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center"
                   Style="{StaticResource SubtitleTextBlockStyle}" />
        <!-- protocol-icon: source-concept glyph as a color emoji (render-model.md § D5);
             no Segoe MDL2 Assets symbol font — the default emoji fallback renders it. -->
        <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="ProtocolIcon"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="{x:Bind ids:Ids.ProtocolIcon}" />
        <!-- The policy editor's door. COLLAPSED where the rail models no room,
             like the class statement in row 1 — collapsed is what "absent"
             means to the driver on WinUI, since a collapsed element carries no
             UIA peer. Where the thread IS a room it is painted, and merely
             GREYED when the viewer's role may not set policy — never hidden
             (`ui/conversations.md` § Architectural rules 5; a policy-less room greys
             it too, having no policy to edit). -->
        <Button Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="2" x:Name="RoomSettingsButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="{x:Bind ids:Ids.ThreadRoomSettingsButton}"
                Content="&#xE713;" FontFamily="Segoe MDL2 Assets"
                Visibility="Collapsed"
                ToolTipService.ToolTip="{loc:Localize Key=conversations/unified/thread_room_settings}"
                Click="OnRoomSettingsClicked" />
        <Button Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="3" x:Name="RenameButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="{x:Bind ids:Ids.ThreadRenameButton}"
                Content="&#xE8AC;" FontFamily="Segoe MDL2 Assets"
                Visibility="Collapsed"
                ToolTipService.ToolTip="{loc:Localize Key=conversations/unified/thread_rename}"
                Click="OnRenameClicked" />
        <Button Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="4" x:Name="OverflowButton"
                Content="&#xE712;" FontFamily="Segoe MDL2 Assets" />
        </Grid>

        <!-- Row 1 — the room's class, stated on the header
             (`conversation-rooms.md` § The three classes) as a caption line of
             its own, full pane width, so it never takes the title's width (the
             top comment has the measurement). COLLAPSED where the rail models no
             room, so `thread-room-class` is genuinely absent off a room rather
             than present-and-empty — and a collapsed element takes no layout
             space, margin included, so a non-room header gains no gap.

             No AutomationProperties.Name on the class label: it carries its
             token in HelpText (the `class` attribute), and a static Name makes
             a dynamic label read back wrong. -->
        <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" x:Name="RoomClassLabel"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="{x:Bind ids:Ids.ThreadRoomClass}"
                   Visibility="Collapsed"
                   Opacity="0.7" FontSize="12"
                   TextWrapping="NoWrap" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                   Margin="0,4,0,0" />

        <!-- Row 2 — the participant chips and the add affordance, in their own
             Grid so their width can never reach row 0's columns. -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="2" Margin="0,4,0,0">
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <ItemsControl Grid.Column="0" x:Name="MembersList">
            <!-- StackPanel(Horizontal): plain ItemsControl can't host a virtualizing
                 panel like ItemsWrapGrid; items silently fail to materialize. -->
            <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate>
                    <!--
                        AutomationId lives on the outer Button — a bare
                        StackPanel/Border has no reliable FlaUI clickable
                        point (measured: NoClickablePointException on this
                        exact chip) — and NOT the label TextBlock: a scoped
                        query (scope="thread-member-chip[i]") resolves as a
                        DESCENDANT match, and a TextBlock is a leaf that can
                        carry no children, so the review pair below could
                        never resolve scoped to it. AutomationProperties.Name
                        keeps the button in the UIA content view so nested
                        child ids stay resolvable (mirrors folder-destination-row
                        / folder-device-activity-item's SetName idiom). The
                        Border inside keeps the exact same pill visual as
                        before the wrap.
                    -->
                    <!--
                        `role` (owner | admin | member) rides HelpText — the
                        bridge maps every attribute but `name`/`disabled` there
                        — and is empty on a policy-less room and on every non-room
                        thread, where there are no roles to mark (NOT "everyone
                        is a member").

                        IsEnabled carries the greying: a chip is the Remove
                        control, so it goes insensitive exactly when the rail's
                        membership is mutable but the viewer's ROLE may not
                        remove (`RemoveEnabled`) — greyed, never hidden, and
                        never demoted to something that reads as inert. A mail
                        chip is NOT that case: its membership is immutable, so
                        it stays a live-looking pill and the handler guard
                        refuses it, which is the stance ThreadHeader.xaml.cs
                        has always documented.

                        ⚠ One consequence, stated rather than discovered later:
                        the post-succession review pair below is INSIDE this
                        Button (it has to be — a scoped query resolves as a
                        descendant match), and WinUI's IsEnabled is inherited,
                        so a greyed chip greys its Keep button too. linux, whose
                        chip greys only the label, keeps Keep live. No test
                        drives Keep today (`actions/conversations.py::keep_member`
                        has no caller), and the two states barely overlap — a
                        plain member of a governed room, with a review item
                        open, on this seat. Un-nesting the pair to fix it would
                        break the scoped-descendant contract the comment above
                        records as measured, so the nesting wins and this is the
                        price.
                    -->
                    <Button Background="Transparent" BorderThickness="0" Padding="0"
                            Margin="0,0,4,0"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="thread-member-chip"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Display}"
                            AutomationProperties.HelpText="{Binding RoleToken}"
                            IsEnabled="{Binding RemoveEnabled}"
                            Tag="{Binding Address}"
                            Click="OnMemberChipClicked">
                        <Border Background="{ThemeResource SubtleFillColorSecondaryBrush}"
                                CornerRadius="10" Padding="6,2">
                            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                                <!-- Capped and ellipsised: an un-announced member
                                     renders as a full 64-hex actor id, which as a
                                     single pill is both unreadable to a human and
                                     wide enough to push everything beside it out of
                                     the pane. The driver is unaffected — the chip's
                                     id and text both come off the Button's
                                     AutomationProperties.Name, which carries the
                                     WHOLE Display. -->
                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Display}" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                           MaxWidth="240"
                                           TextWrapping="NoWrap" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                                <!-- The post-succession review pair, scoped INSIDE this chip
                                     (succession-aftermath.md § Propagation → *Removing a flagged
                                     member*) — present only while the participant carries an open
                                     review item. Remove is deliberately NOT re-rendered: the chip
                                     itself already is it (mirrors tui/linux/apple/web/android). -->
                                <TextBlock AutomationProperties.AutomationId="thread-member-unattested-mark"
                                           Text="{loc:Localize Key=conversations/detail/member_unattested_mark}"
                                           Visibility="{Binding ReviewVisibility}"
                                           Opacity="0.7" FontSize="11"
                                           Margin="4,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                                <Button AutomationProperties.AutomationId="thread-member-keep-button"
                                        Content="{loc:Localize Key=conversations/detail/member_keep}"
                                        Visibility="{Binding ReviewVisibility}"
                                        Tag="{Binding PersonHex}"
                                        Click="OnMemberKeepClicked"
                                        Padding="6,2" FontSize="11"
                                        Margin="2,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                            </StackPanel>
                        </Border>
                    </Button>
                </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>
        <Button x:Name="AddParticipantButton"
                Grid.Column="1"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="{x:Bind ids:Ids.ThreadAddParticipantButton}"
                Content="+"
                Visibility="Collapsed"
                ToolTipService.ToolTip="{loc:Localize Key=conversations/unified/thread_add_participant}"
                Click="OnAddParticipantClicked" />
        </Grid>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
